{
  "version": 1,
  "language": "en",
  "blocks": [
    {"id":"tldr","type":"summary","content":[{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" returns "},{"type":"text","text":"false","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" whenever the two operands have different types, while "},{"type":"text","text":"==","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" first converts one of them using a short, fixed list of rules from the spec. When the types already match, the two operators behave exactly the same. Neither one compares objects by their contents, and neither one treats "},{"type":"text","text":"NaN","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" as equal to itself."}]},
    {"id":"b1","type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A settings page has a \"Max downloads\" field where 0 means unlimited. QA reports that clearing the field makes the summary say \"Unlimited\". The code reads "},{"type":"text","text":"if (limit == 0)","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", and "},{"type":"text","text":"limit","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" comes straight from the input's "},{"type":"text","text":"value","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":". A teammate changes it to "},{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" because the linter complains, and now typing 0 doesn't show \"Unlimited\" either. Neither operator was the bug. The code compared a string with a number and never decided what an empty field means."}]},
    {"id":"b2","type":"heading","level":2,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The common misconception"}]},
    {"id":"b3","type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The belief: "},{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" compares value and type, "},{"type":"text","text":"==","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" compares the value and ignores the type, and that's all there is to equality. Use "},{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" everywhere and the problem goes away."}]},
    {"id":"b4","type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It's a good first rule, and MDN agrees with its conclusion: strict equality is almost always the comparison you want. The weak part is \"ignores the type\". "},{"type":"text","text":"==","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" doesn't ignore types; it converts one operand to match the other in specific steps, and those steps are what make "},{"type":"text","text":"\"\" == 0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" true and "},{"type":"text","text":"\"true\" == true","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" false. And "},{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" doesn't compare \"values\" the way people picture it: two objects with the same contents are not equal, and "},{"type":"text","text":"NaN","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" isn't even equal to itself."}]},
    {"id":"b5","type":"heading","level":2,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What actually happens"}]},
    {"id":"b6","type":"heading","level":3,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When the types match, == is ==="}]},
    {"id":"b7","type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The spec defines "},{"type":"text","text":"==","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" through an abstract operation called IsLooselyEqual. Its very first step says: if both operands have the same type, return IsStrictlyEqual, the operation behind "},{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":". So "},{"type":"text","text":"x == y","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"x === y","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" can only disagree when "},{"type":"text","text":"x","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"y","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" have different types. Comparing two strings, two numbers or two objects with "},{"type":"text","text":"==","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is exactly a "},{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" comparison, with no conversion at all."}]},
    {"id":"b8","type":"heading","level":3,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When they don't, == follows a short list of conversions"}]},
    {"id":"b9","type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For mixed types, IsLooselyEqual matches the pair against a few cases, converts one side, and compares again:"}]},
    {"id":"b10","type":"orderedList","items":[[{"type":"text","text":"null","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"undefined","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" are equal to each other and to nothing else, not even "},{"type":"text","text":"0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"text","text":"false","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}],[{"type":"text","text":"A number against a string: the string becomes a number. An empty or whitespace-only string becomes "},{"type":"text","text":"0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", and something like "},{"type":"text","text":"\"12px\"","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" becomes "},{"type":"term","termId":"nan","text":"NaN"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],[{"type":"text","text":"A boolean on either side becomes a number first: "},{"type":"text","text":"true","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is "},{"type":"text","text":"1","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"false","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is "},{"type":"text","text":"0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}],[{"type":"text","text":"An object against a primitive: the object is converted to a primitive. An array becomes its comma-joined string, so "},{"type":"text","text":"[]","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" becomes "},{"type":"text","text":"\"\"","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}],[{"type":"text","text":"A BigInt is compared with a number by mathematical value, and with a string after parsing the string as a BigInt."}],[{"type":"text","text":"Any other mix, such as a symbol against a string, is not equal."}]]},
    {"id":"b11","type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rule 3 surprises people most. "},{"type":"text","text":"\"true\" == true","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" doesn't ask whether the string looks truthy: "},{"type":"text","text":"true","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" becomes "},{"type":"text","text":"1","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", then "},{"type":"text","text":"\"true\"","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" becomes "},{"type":"text","text":"NaN","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", and "},{"type":"text","text":"NaN","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" equals nothing. This automatic conversion is called "},{"type":"term","termId":"type-coercion","text":"type coercion"},{"type":"text","text":", and it explains every \"weird\" "},{"type":"text","text":"==","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" result people love to share."}]},
    {"id":"b12","type":"heading","level":3,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== has its own rules for numbers and objects"}]},
    {"id":"b13","type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"IsStrictlyEqual returns "},{"type":"text","text":"false","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" for different types. For two numbers it uses numeric equality, where "},{"type":"text","text":"NaN","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is not equal to any value, itself included, and "},{"type":"text","text":"+0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" equals "},{"type":"text","text":"-0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":". For everything else it checks whether both sides are the same value, and for objects that means the same object in memory, known as "},{"type":"term","termId":"referential-equality","text":"referential equality"},{"type":"text","text":". Two "},{"type":"text","text":"{ id: 1 }","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" literals are two different objects, with "},{"type":"text","text":"==","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},
    {"id":"b14","type":"code","lang":"js","filename":"equality.js","code":"console.log(\"\" == 0); // true: \"\" converts to the number 0\nconsole.log(\"true\" == true); // false: true → 1, \"true\" → NaN\nconsole.log([] == false); // true: [] → \"\" → 0, false → 0\nconsole.log(null == 0); // false: null only equals undefined\nconsole.log(NaN === NaN); // false: NaN equals nothing\nconsole.log({ id: 1 } === { id: 1 }); // false: two different objects\nconsole.log([NaN].indexOf(NaN)); // -1: indexOf uses ===\nconsole.log([NaN].includes(NaN)); // true: includes uses SameValueZero"},
    {"id":"b15","type":"heading","level":3,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Four comparisons, not two"}]},
    {"id":"b16","type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The last two lines show that the language has more than two kinds of equality. MDN lists four algorithms, and built-in methods pick different ones. That is why "},{"type":"text","text":"indexOf","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" can't find "},{"type":"text","text":"NaN","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" but "},{"type":"text","text":"includes","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" can:"}]},
    {"id":"b17","type":"table","header":[[{"type":"text","text":"Algorithm"}],[{"type":"text","text":"Used by"}],[{"type":"text","text":"NaN","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" vs "},{"type":"text","text":"NaN","marks":[{"type":"code"}]}],[{"type":"text","text":"+0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" vs "},{"type":"text","text":"-0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]}],[{"type":"text","text":"Converts types?"}]],"rows":[[[{"type":"text","text":"IsLooselyEqual"}],[{"type":"text","text":"==","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"!=","marks":[{"type":"code"}]}],[{"type":"text","text":"not equal"}],[{"type":"text","text":"equal"}],[{"type":"text","text":"yes"}]],[[{"type":"text","text":"IsStrictlyEqual"}],[{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"!==","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"indexOf","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"switch","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" cases"}],[{"type":"text","text":"not equal"}],[{"type":"text","text":"equal"}],[{"type":"text","text":"no"}]],[[{"type":"text","text":"SameValueZero"}],[{"type":"text","text":"includes","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"Map","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"Set","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" keys"}],[{"type":"text","text":"equal"}],[{"type":"text","text":"equal"}],[{"type":"text","text":"no"}]],[[{"type":"text","text":"SameValue"}],[{"type":"text","text":"Object.is","marks":[{"type":"code"}]}],[{"type":"text","text":"equal"}],[{"type":"text","text":"not equal"}],[{"type":"text","text":"no"}]]]},
    {"id":"b18","type":"callout","variant":"info","title":"Relational operators play by other rules","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":">","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"<=","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":">=","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" never use IsLooselyEqual. They convert both sides to primitives and, unless both are strings, compare them as numbers, where "},{"type":"text","text":"null","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" becomes "},{"type":"text","text":"0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":". That's why "},{"type":"text","text":"null >= 0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is "},{"type":"text","text":"true","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" while "},{"type":"text","text":"null == 0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is "},{"type":"text","text":"false","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":". Don't infer one result from the other."}]},
    {"id":"b19","type":"heading","level":3,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fixing the settings bug"}]},
    {"id":"b20","type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Back to the download limit. An input's "},{"type":"text","text":"value","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is always a string, so "},{"type":"text","text":"limit == 0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" matches "},{"type":"text","text":"\"0\"","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"\"\"","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"\"  \"","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":". Switching to "},{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" makes it match nothing, because a string is never "},{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" a number. The fix isn't a different operator. It's converting once at the edge and deciding what an empty field means:"}]},
    {"id":"b21","type":"codeTabs","tabs":[{"label":"❌ Wrong","lang":"js","filename":"limit.js","code":"// limit comes from an <input>'s value, so it is always a string\nfunction describeLimit(limit) {\n  if (limit == 0) return \"Unlimited\"; // \"0\", \"\" and \"  \" all match\n  return `${limit} downloads`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(describeLimit(\"0\")); // Unlimited\nconsole.log(describeLimit(\"\")); // Unlimited, but the field is empty\nconsole.log(describeLimit(\"5\")); // 5 downloads"},{"label":"✅ Right","lang":"js","filename":"limit.js","code":"// convert once at the edge, then compare like with like\nfunction parseLimit(text) {\n  if (text.trim() === \"\") return null; // empty means \"not set\"\n  const n = Number(text);\n  return Number.isInteger(n) && n >= 0 ? n : null;\n}\n\nfunction describeLimit(limit) {\n  if (limit === null) return \"Not set\";\n  if (limit === 0) return \"Unlimited\";\n  return `${limit} downloads`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(describeLimit(parseLimit(\"0\"))); // Unlimited\nconsole.log(describeLimit(parseLimit(\"\"))); // Not set\nconsole.log(describeLimit(parseLimit(\"5\"))); // 5 downloads"}]},
    {"id":"b22","type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" in the second version isn't doing anything clever. It works because both sides are already numbers or "},{"type":"text","text":"null","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", so there is no conversion left to surprise anyone."}]},
    {"id":"b23","type":"callout","variant":"tip","title":"== null is the one loose check worth knowing","content":[{"type":"text","text":"value == null","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is true for "},{"type":"text","text":"null","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"undefined","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and for nothing else (rule 1), so it reads as \"is this value missing?\" in one comparison. If your team bans "},{"type":"text","text":"==","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" entirely, "},{"type":"text","text":"value === null || value === undefined","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" says the same thing, and "},{"type":"text","text":"??","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" covers the common \"use a default when it's missing\" case."}]},
    {"id":"b24","type":"callout","variant":"warning","title":"Don't bulk-replace == with ===","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Swapping operators across a codebase is not a safe refactor: every "},{"type":"text","text":"==","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" that compared different types now returns "},{"type":"text","text":"false","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":". Before changing one, check where each side comes from. Values from inputs, URLs, "},{"type":"text","text":"localStorage","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"dataset","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" are strings, so convert them first, or the swap turns a coercion bug into a \"never equal\" bug."}]},
    {"id":"b25","type":"heading","level":2,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to apply it in your project"}]},
    {"id":"b26","type":"steps","items":[{"title":"Convert at the boundary","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Turn form values, query parameters and storage values into the type you mean, with "},{"type":"text","text":"Number()","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" or an explicit check for "},{"type":"text","text":"\"\"","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", right where they enter your code."}]},{"title":"Default to ===","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Once types are settled, "},{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" says exactly what you mean. Keep "},{"type":"text","text":"== null","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" only where it clearly means \"missing\"."}]},{"title":"Use the right tool for NaN and zeros","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Use "},{"type":"text","text":"Number.isNaN(x)","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" to detect "},{"type":"text","text":"NaN","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"includes","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" instead of "},{"type":"text","text":"indexOf","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" when an array may hold "},{"type":"text","text":"NaN","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", and "},{"type":"text","text":"Object.is","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" when the sign of zero matters."}]},{"title":"Compare objects by their fields","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Neither operator looks inside objects. Compare the field that defines identity, like an "},{"type":"text","text":"id","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", or write a small function that compares the fields you care about."}]}]},
    {"id":"b27","type":"heading","level":2,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Key takeaways"}]},
    {"id":"b28","type":"bulletList","items":[[{"type":"text","text":"==","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" behave identically when both operands have the same type; they only differ on mixed types."}],[{"type":"text","text":"==","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" converts in specific steps: "},{"type":"text","text":"null","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"undefined","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" only match each other, booleans and strings become numbers, objects become primitives."}],[{"type":"text","text":"===","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" isn't just \"value and type\": "},{"type":"text","text":"NaN","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" never equals itself, "},{"type":"text","text":"+0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" equals "},{"type":"text","text":"-0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", and objects compare by identity."}],[{"type":"text","text":"Object.is","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"includes","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"Map","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"Set","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" use their own sameness rules, so "},{"type":"text","text":"NaN","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"-0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" behave differently there."}],[{"type":"text","text":"Fix comparisons by converting inputs once at the edge; swapping the operator alone just moves the bug."}]]}
  ]
}
